<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manica, Mozambique</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Macequece, Masekesa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manica, formerly Macequece, is a town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. It grew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masekesa">Masekesa</a>, a Portuguese gold-trading fair in the Manyika highlands. In May 1891 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> forces defeated Portuguese troops there, a clash followed by the Anglo-Portuguese treaty that left the town in Mozambique.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manica-mozambique.jpg" alt="Manica, Mozambique" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Senorhorst Jahnsen from Mumbai, India · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manica_Centro_da_Cidade_-_old_Church_(6555271555).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
